# Chapter Five — The Window

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. This chapter crosses the opening-act murder threshold. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction or investigation branches yet.

## Scene 8 — The call

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Lydia, Edwin, Marian, Robert, Nell.

= Immediate pressure: Nell is close to going downstairs after Paul. Ruth needs the room to decide that his departure is ordinary.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

Robert is halfway through his second cup when the telephone rings at the service bar.

Edwin answers it.

EDWIN
Winter Room.

He listens.

EDWIN
Slowly, Mr. Bell.

Everyone is looking at him now.

EDWIN
Is the person moving?

He listens again.

EDWIN
Call the police from receiving. Do not open anything. I am coming down.

He hangs up.

NELL
What happened?

EDWIN
Mr. Bell says there is a man inside the unfinished window.

No one moves at first.

ROBERT
One of the workers?

EDWIN
He believes it is Mr. Ellery.

Nell goes toward the service lift. Edwin reaches it first.

EDWIN
Wait here.

NELL
No.

EDWIN
The display is locked. Let me find out what he saw.

NELL
You already asked if he was moving.

Edwin cannot answer that without answering something else.

Henry takes his coat from the chair.

HENRY
Has Bell called the police?

EDWIN
He will have by now.

HENRY
We find out whether Paul can be helped. If he can't, nobody touches anything until they arrive.

EDWIN
Come with me.

HENRY
You don't need a private investigator.

EDWIN
I need someone who knows what not to touch.

Edwin takes the brass key from his jacket.

Ruth stands.

RUTH
Henry.

He looks at her.

He enters the lift with Edwin. Lydia steps between Nell and the opening. The wire-glass door closes.

The lift descends.

Through the panel, dark departments pass in sections: the empty lamps, the canvas over the toys, the furniture arranged into rooms.

Edwin turns the brass key at the cosmetics floor. The lift continues one short level lower.

## Scene 9 — The motor court

= Present: Henry, Edwin, Mr. Bell; Paul visible inside the display.

= Immediate pressure: Bell has found something he cannot make sense of. Henry must separate what the window resembles from what happened inside it.

INT./EXT. TOLLAND'S, DISPLAY VESTIBULE AND MOTOR COURT - NIGHT

The lift opens onto a narrow vestibule behind the motor court. Mr. Bell is waiting there in a gray guard's uniform. He is perhaps sixty and has forgotten to put on his cap.

BELL
Mr. Price.

EDWIN
Show me.

Bell leads them through the service door and into the motor court.

The night air is colder at street level. The lamps beneath the stone canopy shine into the display glass.

Six mannequins remain where Henry saw them on the way in. They face the empty seventh chair. Behind it, the white paper moon has split and sagged inward.

There is a man beneath it.

His body lies partly behind the chair, one shoulder against its steel base. A shoe extends into the light. One hand rests palm down on the display floor. His clothing has the softness the mannequins' clothing lacks.

Bell stops several feet from the glass.

BELL
I thought they'd finished it.

EDWIN
Finished what?

BELL
The window. I thought they'd put another figure in.

Henry steps closer to the glass.

The face is Paul's.

HENRY
Where is the entrance?

Bell takes a ring of keys from his belt and unlocks a narrow door beside his booth. It opens onto the display stage.

BELL
This is the only door down here.

HENRY
Was it locked?

BELL
Yes.

HENRY
How do you know?

BELL
Because I opened it.

Henry looks at him.

BELL
Only far enough to see his face. Then I locked it and called upstairs.

HENRY
Who else has that key?

BELL
Mr. Price. The display chief in the morning.

Edwin removes a smaller key from a second ring and holds it where Henry can see it.

EDWIN
My display-stage key has not left me.

Henry opens the stage door. Edwin and Bell remain outside.

The display lights give off a low electrical hum. Paper moves slightly where the moon has torn.

Henry kneels beside Paul. He touches two fingers beneath the jaw, waits, then withdraws them.

He does not move the body.

EDWIN
Mr. Calder?

Henry looks at Edwin and shakes his head once.

Bell reaches for his cap, remembers it is not there, and lowers his hand.

Henry looks up.

The paper moon hangs from a scenic wall that rises to the cosmetics floor. Near the top, an open section of gallery rail makes a black rectangle. A shallow carton sits on the gallery beside it.

Beneath the steel lip are pale horizontal marks. Lower down, one long scrape crosses the painted wall.

A red button is mounted beside the open rail.

Henry looks back at Paul.

A torn piece of typed paper, creased where his hand closed on it, remains beneath the fingers of his right hand. Henry does not pull it free. Enough of the top edge is visible to read:

!INT. BEDROOM — NIGHT

It is not a letter.

Henry leaves the display and Bell locks the door again.

HENRY
Tell me what happened when he came downstairs.

BELL
Mr. Ellery came out of the lift with his sister.

HENRY
Ruth Ellery.

BELL
Yes, sir. I gave him the carton. He needed both hands, so he gave her the lift key. They were beside the display-access door when I went into receiving to make the closing call. Then I crossed the court to check the windows.

EDWIN
Did you open the motor-court door for him?

BELL
No.

HENRY
Did he ask you to?

BELL
No, sir.

HENRY
When did you see him again?

Bell looks through the glass.

BELL
There.

HENRY
And Miss Ellery?

BELL
Not after I gave them the carton.

Henry turns toward the service entrance. From the vestibule, the lift is on the left. On the right is a plain door marked DISPLAY ACCESS.

HENRY
Is that door locked?

BELL
Not while a window is unfinished.

HENRY
Where does it go?

Bell points upward.

BELL
There is a short stair behind it. That goes to the gallery.

Through the wired-glass pane in the access door, Henry can see the enclosed stair turn upward behind the scenery. Something white has been tracked down the lower treads. At the top of the stair is the same black opening above Paul's body. The shallow carton sits just beyond it.

A distant police siren becomes audible beyond the stone canopy.

Edwin looks at the brass key in his hand.

EDWIN
Mr. Bell never opened the door.

HENRY
No.

Through the glass, the six mannequins continue facing the seventh place.

Henry has attended the supper for less than two hours. For the first time that evening, he is working.
